    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d368">

                <pb facs="0533" n="453" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg453"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.C1199/193: Telegram (part air)</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gilbert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-05-26T18:00:00+01:00">May 26, 1936—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 27—11:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>195. Consulate’s 631, November 30, [1935,] 2 p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d368-f1"/>
                </p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Secretariat is today notifying members of the Economic Committee that next
                        session will begin July 7<anchor n="2" xml:id="d368-f2"/> and that question
                        of most-favored-nation clause will be placed on agenda. Secretariat note on
                        this subject will probably be available in about 10 days.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Stoppani<anchor n="3" xml:id="d368-f3"/> informs me it appears unlikely
                        that a separate technical committee will be set up. He states confidentially
                        he is anxious to arrange that American policy be fully presented and
                        suggests (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) that Rogers<anchor n="4"
                            xml:id="d368-f4"/> might wish to name as substitute for July meeting
                        some one such as Grady<anchor n="5" xml:id="d368-f5"/> who is fully familiar
                        with American policy, (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) that Rogers be
                        accompanied by some such expert (in which case no invitation would be issued
                        and expenses would not be paid by the League), (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >c</hi>) that if Department so desired Stoppani might be able to arrange
                        for the Economic Committee to invite two experts to attend July meeting
                        having in mind Rist (France) and an American, their expenses to be paid by
                        the League.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>From what I can gather the initiative in the project to consider the
                        most-favored-nation principle lies chiefly with the Secretariat. While I
                        will keep you as informed as possible I do not anticipate that I shall be
                        able to ascertain in advance how the matter may develop in the Committee or
                        what positions may be taken. Stoppani states his conviction, however, that
                        the attitude of the Committee will be to uphold the most-favored-nation
                        principle. Please instruct.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d368-f1" n="1" xml:id="d368-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d368-f2" n="2" xml:id="d368-f2-content">
                <p>The session was postponed until September 7, 1936.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d368-f3" n="3" xml:id="d368-f3-content">
                <p>Pietro Stoppani, Director of the Economic Relations Section, League of Nations
                    Secretariat.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d368-f4" n="4" xml:id="d368-f4-content">
                <p>James Harvey Rogers, professor of political economy, Yale University, and member
                    of the Economic Committee of the League of Nations.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d368-f5" n="5" xml:id="d368-f5-content">
                <p>Henry F. Grady, Dean of the College of Commerce and professor of international
                    trade, University of California at Berkeley.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
